The core programming language I'll be using to build Prototype 2.0 is Python 3.12. I chose Python 3.12 because it sits in a sweet spot:
it delivers major speed improvements over older versions while remaining completely compatible with the entire data science, machine 
learning, and financial programming ecosystem, meaning zero dependency headaches when scaling up later.

The development environment is going to be Visual Studio Code. Beyond just writing code, VS Code gives me integrated debugging and a terminal access, virtual environment management, and instant linting so I can catch syntax or logic errors before running a single test.

Git and GitHub will act as the "bookkeeper" where I control versions and project history. Every time I test a new trading strategy, tweak an indicator, or fix a bug, Git tracks the exact changes, allowing me to experiment safely on separate branches and roll back immediately if a new idea underperforms.

pandas  will be used for financial data analysis. It allows me to structure raw market prices into clean time-series tables (DataFrames), align timestamps, handle missing market candles, and calculate rolling technical indicators like moving averages without writing messy code loops.

NumPy will handle the heavy mathematical calculations. Under the hood, it runs fast, vectorized array math, which is essential for 
calculating returns, volatility, signal thresholds, and statistical metrics across thousands of data points in milliseconds.

Requests will be used for gathering external information from REST APIs. Whenever I need data outside of my primary broker such as 
macroeconomic calendars, news headlines, or alternative market feeds. Requests handles the HTTP calls, headers, and JSON parsing cleanly.

Python-dotenv will be used for securely managing API credentials. Instead of hardcoding sensitive broker keys directly into my scripts
where they could accidentally be exposed on GitHub, this library loads them privately from a local .env file at runtime.

Pytest is what I'll be using for testing my code. In algorithmic trading, a single math bug or unhandled edge case can cause bad trades;
Pytest lets me build automated checks to verify that my signal logic, order sizing, and risk rules behave exactly as expected before
going live.

alpaca-py is the official SDK I'll be using for stock and cryptocurrency integration. It connects my Python logic directly to Alpaca's
market data streams and trading engine, letting me test strategies safely in a paper-trading sandbox before transitioning to live execution.

Once this core foundation is solid, I'll be expanding prototype 2.0 by introducing FinBERT for financial news sentiment analysis, additional AI models, custom web scraping tools, and multi-broker integrations later on.
